33,0.028,0.084,0.044,0.016,0.017,0.048,0.032,0.041,0.076,0.034,0.052,0.05,0.07,0.068,0.059,0.065,0.079,0.047,0.008,0.014,0.01,0.034,0.024,0.091,0.031,0.068,0.041,0.022,0.016,0.029,0.028,0.008,0.013,0.013,0.053,0.009,0.007,0.033,0.013,0.002,0,0.019,0.054,0.048,0.046,0.062,0.1,0.018,0.062,0.096,0.06,0.036,0.019,0.011,0.033,0.056,0.027,0.008,0.024,0.185,0.078,0.036,0.01,0.004,0.015,0.011,0.031,0.031,0.072,0.065,0.064,0.029,0.062,0.04,0.021,0.015,0.047,0.128,0.094,0.045,0.064,0.052,0.101,0.027,0.005,0.078,0.036,0.031,0.066,0.06,0.095,0.077,0.024,0.085,0.127,0.028,0.041,0.087,0.027,0.075,0.039,0.024,0.007,0.008,0.046,0.001,0.007,0.031,0.105,0.061,0.049,0.056,0.054,0.161,0.046,0.033,0.037,0.023,0.009,0.01,0.056,0.054,0.022,0.045,0.041,0.05,0.04,0.123,0.045,0.062,0.052,0.044,0.029,0.064,0.015,0.085,0.088,0.097,0.066,0.024,0.022,0.017,0.052,0.039,0.067,0.014,0.028,0.04,0.038,0.111,0.048,0.023,0.047,0.011,0.031,0.03,0.027,0.042,0.029,0.059,0.014]}},"alerts":[{"event":"Hydrologic Outlook","headline":"Hydrologic Outlook issued September 27 at 1:01PM MDT by NWS Grand Junction CO","description":"ESFGJT\n\nStrong southwesterly winds ahead of a closed low centered over\nCalifornia will pull tropical moisture from Tropical Storm Odalys and\nHurricane Polo towards eastern Utah and western Colorado, with\natmospheric moisture peaking in our area on Monday. The northerly\nextent of the expected excessive rainfall poses a threat to our\nsouthern San Juan river basins. Current forecasts suggest 1 to 4\ninches of rain Monday morning through early Wednesday. At face value,\nrivers and tributaries will see a rise due to the forecast rainfall.\nHowever, localized heavier amounts could result in flooding across\nthe southern San Juan foothills and communities further downstream.\n\nRivers have responded rapidly to recent rainfall due to saturated\nsoils and increased runoff potential. Thus, prolonged periods of\nadditional rainfall over these regions would trigger a similar\nresponse. The current precipitation forecast suggests larger peaks in\nseveral creeks and rivers than what has been recently observed.\nHowever, high end probabilistic rainfall amounts (i.e. above 90th\npercentile) may lead to rivers exceeding action stage to minor\nflooding. Most probable basins to see significant rises include; the\nRio Blanco, San Juan, Piedra, Los Pinos/Vallecito Creek, and the\nAnimas River Basins.\n\nConvective storms and periods of heavy rainfall fed from said\ntropical moisture could result in localized flooding impacts across\nthe CWA, however, confidence decreases rapidly north of the San\nJuans. Confidence has increased enough for a Flood Watch to be issued\nfor southern portions of the forecast area valid from 6am Monday\nthrough 6am Wednesday. Continue to monitor the latest forecasts\nespecially for those who reside and/or plan to recreate in the Watch\nareas.","ends":"2026-09-28T15:15:00-06:00","severity":"Unknown","areaDesc":"Archuleta, CO; Dolores, CO; Gunnison, CO; Hinsdale, CO; La Plata, CO; Mesa, CO; Montezuma, CO; Montrose, CO; Ouray, CO; San Juan, CO; San Miguel, CO; Grand, UT; San Juan, UT","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.7d95a2138492d35262c0408a70acac4faa29c1f7.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.7d95a2138492d35262c0408a70acac4faa29c1f7.001.1"},{"event":"Hydrologic Outlook","headline":"Hydrologic Outlook issued September 25 at 2:21PM MDT by NWS Grand Junction CO","description":"ESFGJT\n\nIn the wake of today's precipitation, our attention will turn to the\nEastern Pacific. Tropical moisture is anticipated to spread across\nthe Desert Southwest early next week, drawing moisture from a pair of\nHurricanes; Odalys and Polo. The forecast becomes increasing complex\ndue to a closed low currently forecast to drive across the Southwest\nCONUS as Hurricane Polo approaches the Baja California Peninsula.\nStrong southerly winds ahead of the low are expected to pull tropical\nmoisture towards eastern Utah and western Colorado as early as Monday\nmorning. The challenge and uncertainty in our precipitation forecast\nincrease as the week progresses and the low traverses our southern\nStates. Excessive rainfall is most probable over AZ and NM, attm, but\nthe northerly extent poses a threat to our southern San Juan river\nbasins. Current forecasts suggests 1 to 3 inches of rain over Monday\nmorning through Wednesday. At face value, rivers and tributaries will\nsee a bump up due to said moisture. However, localized heavier\namounts could result in minor flooding across the southern San Juan\nfoothills and communities further downstream.\n\nRivers have responded rapidly to recent rainfall due to saturated\nsoils and increased runoff potential. Thus, prolonged periods of\nadditional rainfall over these regions would trigger a similar\nresponse. The current precipitation forecast suggests larger peaks in\nseveral creeks and rivers than what has been observed over the\nprevious 7 days. However, high end probabilistic rainfall amounts\n(i.e. above 90th percentile) may lead to rivers exceeding action\nstage to minor flooding. Most probable basins to see significant\nrises include; the Rio Blanco, San Juan, Piedra, Los Pinos/Vallecito\nCreek, and the Animas River Basins.\n\nConvective storms and periods of heavy rainfall fed from said\ntropical moisture could result in localized flooding impacts across\nthe CWA, however, confidence decreases rapidly north of the San\nJuans. Please continue to monitor the forecast as these systems\nevolve. Especially for those who reside and/or plan to recreate in\nthe southwest quadrant of the forecast area.","ends":"2026-09-30T06:00:00-06:00","severity":"Unknown","areaDesc":"Archuleta, CO; Dolores, CO; Gunnison, CO; Hinsdale, CO; La Plata, CO; Montezuma, CO; Ouray, CO; San Juan, CO; San Miguel, CO; Grand, UT; San Juan, UT","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.ce67801ec73048f07c44dd0418c6a50edd1c8fdc.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.ce67801ec73048f07c44dd0418c6a50edd1c8fdc.001.1"}],"afd":{"office":"BOU","issued":"2026-09-27T21:05:00+00:00","snippet":"000 FXUS65 KBOU 272105 AFDBOU Area Forecast Discussion National Weather Service Denver/Boulder CO 305 PM MDT Sun Sep 27 2026 .KEY MESSAGES... - Warm today, but isolated to scattered showers and storms with gusty winds this afternoon and evening. - Potential for heavy rain Wednesday and Thursday, but confidence in amounts and locations remains low. && .DISCUSSION /Through Sunday/... Issued at 302 P","url":"https://forecast.weather.gov/product.php?site=BOU&issuedby=BOU&product=AFD&format=CI"},"hwo":null,"fwf":{"office":"BOU","issued":"2026-09-27T20:21:00+00:00","snippet":".DISCUSSION...Tonight through Friday... Southerly breezes will occur over the plains east of I-25 tonight through Monday, while light winds prevail elsewhere. Isolated to scattered high based showers will produce mostly light rain or sprinkles, along with gusty outflow winds to 30-40 mph. Good to very good humidity recovery is expected tonight. Humidity values for Monday will be higher as a push o","url":"https://forecast.weather.gov/product.php?site=BOU&issuedby=BOU&product=FWF&format=CI"},"coagmet":{"station_id":"gby01","station_name":"Granby","distance_km":2.1,"soil_temp_5cm_f":67.96,"soil_temp_15cm_f":60.66,"soil_moisture_5cm":null,"soil_moisture_15cm":null,"precip_in":null,"eto_in":null,"vapor_pressure":null,"solar_radiation":44.58,"wind_speed_mph":8.51,"air_temp_f":59.86,"relative_humidity":0.493,"url":"https://coagmet.colostate.edu/station/gby01"},"aviation":{"icao":"KAEJ","distance_km":0,"raw_metar":"METAR KAEJ 272355Z AUTO 18005KT 10SM SCT100 SCT120 19/00 A3028 RMK AO2 T01880001 10227 20188","temp_f":66,"wind_dir":180,"wind_kt":5,"gust_kt":null,"visibility":"10+","cover":"SCT","flight_category":"VFR","altimeter":1025.5,"observed":1790553300,"raw_taf":null,"url":"https://aviationweather.gov/data/metar/?ids=KAEJ&hours=0"},"airgradient":{"pm25":6,"aqi_pm25":25,"sensor_count":2,"dis